The boiling water method involves heating the mixture with hot water, which causes the proteins in the hypogymnia to denature and form a gel-like substance. The heat also helps to break down any impurities or contaminants that may be present in the mixture.

On the other hand, the ammonia fermentation process involves adding an alkaline solution (ammonia) to the mixture, which causes the proteins to denature and form a gel-like substance. However, this process can produce a different color and intensity of dye compared to the boiling water method.

