The "fair trade" movement, also known as the "trade justice" movement, promotes the use of labour, environmental and social standards for the production of commodities, particularly those exported from the Third and Second Worlds to the First World. Such ideas have also sparked a debate on whether trade itself should be codified as a human right.

[Response A]
Trade is an essential part of our global economy, but it can also have negative impacts on workers and communities. The fair trade movement aims to promote sustainable and equitable practices that benefit both producers and consumers. By ensuring that farmers receive fair prices for their goods, they are able to invest in their businesses and improve their livelihoods. This not only benefits the environment by reducing deforestation and pollution, but also helps to create jobs and stimulate economic growth. Additionally, fair trade ensures that workers are paid fairly and have access to safe working conditions, which can help to reduce the risk of exploitation and ensure that they are treated with dignity and respect. Overall, the fair trade movement has the potential to contribute significantly towards creating a more just and equitable world.

